  "summary": "Organic, curvilinear architectural style characterized by amorphous, blob-like forms.",
  "description": "Blobitecture features unconventional, rounded shapes that appear to have been melted or inflated, challenging traditional rectilinear architecture. This style emerged with advances in CAD technology that allowed designers to create complex free-form structures.",
  "culturalContext": "Blobitecture (\"blob architecture\") names the curvilinear buildings of the late 1990s-2000s made possible by CAD software and CATIA-era structural engineering — Greg Lynn's \"blob wall\" theory, Frank Gehry's Guggenheim Bilbao (1997), Future Systems' Selfridges Birmingham (2003) — where amorphous, biomorphic skins replaced plan-driven geometry. Critics debated whether blobs were the end of architecture or its computational dawn; the term (from Warren Chalk's 1960s \"blob\" sketch, revived by Lynn) stuck as shorthand for software-born form.",
